narrative:
## Taking Control of Drop

`ManuallyDrop[T]` prevents automatic dropping, giving you manual control over when (or if) a value is freed.

### Creating ManuallyDrop

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(2);

let wrapper = ManuallyDrop.new(v);
// v is now inside wrapper — won't be dropped automatically
// You must handle cleanup manually
```

### Why ManuallyDrop?

- **FFI**: C code expects to manage memory
- **Unsafe optimizations**: reusing memory
- **Custom allocators**: bypass default drop
- **Double-buffer patterns**: manually control deallocation

### Taking Value Out

```xiom
let wrapper = ManuallyDrop.new(Vec[Int]());

// Take ownership back
let v = ManuallyDrop.into_inner(wrapper);
// Now v will be dropped normally at end of scope
```

### Dropping Manually

```xiom
let wrapper = ManuallyDrop.new(Vec[Int]());

unsafe {
  ManuallyDrop.drop(wrapper);  // manual drop
}
// wrapper is invalid after manual drop
```

### Safety Contract

When using `ManuallyDrop`, you MUST ensure:
- The value is eventually dropped (or validly moved out)
- No use after manual drop
- No double drop

### Common Pattern: FFI Memory

```xiom
extern "C" {
  fn c_allocate() -> *mut Unit;
  fn c_free(ptr: *mut Unit) -> Unit;
}

fn allocate_wrapper() -> ManuallyDrop[Vec[Int]] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  // C code will free this — don't drop it normally
  return ManuallyDrop.new(v);
}
```

